# FigDown — figures as text. Spec: https://github.com/FigDown/figdown

# Reference figure for genre `table` — NORMATIVE surface only (spec/genres/table.md).
# Every construct here is inside the v0.1 conformance surface and the
# compatibility promise. Experimental constructs are in table-experimental.fd.
# The set demonstrates every FORM of every keyword, not just every keyword.
# Coverage: node tools/reference-coverage.js --normative-only --strict table
figdown 0.1 table
title "Egress scheduler profile"

# `class` declares a meaning ONCE; cells join it with class=. Use it rather
# than a bare fill= whenever the mark MEANS something (`CATEGORICAL-MEANING-MAPPING`): the class label is
# what a reading agent gets instead of a hex value.
class review "Re-measured — awaiting sign-off"        fill=#fce7f3
# A class reaches a CELL through `fill=` and `stroke=` only. `style=` cannot
# reach one by any route: it left `cell`'s option list (`STYLE-KEY-SCOPE`),
# and on a class it is silently ignored for cell members while the legend
# still draws the dash — a swatch for ink no cell can carry
# (`tools/legend-check.js` is the gate for that).
class vendor "Vendor default, not from this profile"  stroke=#9333ea
class capped "Hard ceiling — never oversubscribed"    fill=#dbeafe

table qos "Per-queue scheduling and drop policy"
# Every pipe row ABOVE the delimiter row is a header tier, addressed h1..hN
# top-down. `||` is colspan-LEFT: the empty segment merges into the cell on
# its left, so "Scheduling" covers Class and Weight. It is a line error in the
# first column, where there is nothing to its left.
| Queue | Scheduling || Drop      | Notes |
# `^^` is rowspan-UP. It is a line error in the very first row (nothing above
# to merge into) and in the first DATA row (a span never crosses the delimiter
# row), but legal BETWEEN header tiers — which is what holds Queue and Notes
# upright across both tiers here.
| ^^    | Class | Weight | Threshold | ^^ |
# The delimiter row is REQUIRED (GFM's own term) and is where the header ends
# and data row 1 begins. Its colons set per-column alignment: `:---` left,
# `:--:` centre, `---:` right, bare `---` takes the GFM default — data left,
# headers always centred.
|:------|:-----:|-------:|:----------|-----|
# Data rows are 1-based below the delimiter row. `<br>`, `<br/>` and `<br />`
# are the ONLY in-cell line break, case-insensitive, and become one real
# newline in the model. `\n` in a pipe cell is two literal characters: a pipe
# cell is raw GFM text, not a §1 quoted string.
| Q7 | strict | —  | 100%       | control plane<br>never dropped |
| Q5 | wfq    | 40 | 80%        | video<BR/>voice |
# `\^^` at the START of a cell is the literal caret pair and `\|` anywhere in
# a cell is a literal pipe. Those two are the whole pipe-row escape set — and
# `\^^` is an escape at cell start only, because that is the only place a bare
# `^^` would have meant rowspan.
| Q3 | ^^     | 25 | 80%        | \^^ in the vendor sheet |
| Q1 | ^^     | 20 | 60% \| 90% | best effort |
| Q0 | ^^     | 15 | 40%        | scavenger<br />drop first |
# `width` is ONE whitespace-free comma token, one value per column: `auto`
# (natural), a bare number (px), or a percentage OF the natural total. A `px`
# suffix and the retired space form are both line errors, so this spelling and
# the `width=` option on the layout-zone `pin` share one value grammar.
width auto,70,60,110,auto
# Option position is free on every directive (`OPTION-POSITION-PARSING`) — before the address or
# after. A spanned cell is addressed at its ANCHOR only: `cell (h1,3)` and
# `cell (3,2)` are line errors here — `||` and `^^` merged those away.
cell fill=#dbeafe (h1,2)
cell (h2,4) class=capped
# `highlight` is the ROW form: a bare row number, no comma, data rows only.
cell 1 highlight
# class= takes a comma-separated LIST, applied left to right and later wins
# per channel; these two touch different channels, so the fill comes from
# `review` and the outline colour from `vendor`.
cell (2,5) class=review,vendor
# `style=` left `cell` (`STYLE-KEY-SCOPE`): a dash on one cell carried no
# meaning the table did not already carry, 0 documents outside this repository
# ever wrote it, and it moved with `field`'s removal as one minimum set. So a
# cell cannot be dashed AT ALL — not directly, and not through a class either.
# Reach for a fill or an outline colour when a cell must be marked.

# No label written: the model records it ABSENT and the renderer falls back to
# the id (`OMITTED-LABEL-RECORDING`) — never invent a caption. `fill=` on the block is the default
# DATA-cell fill; the header tint is structural and is not affected by it.
table capacity fill=#fafaf9
| Chassis | Queues per port | Shared buffer |
|---------|----------------:|--------------:|
| C1      |               8 |         32 MB |
| C2      |              16 |         64 MB |
width auto,45%,45%
cell (h1,2) class=capped

# The third label state: WRITTEN EMPTY (`EMPTY-LABEL-STATE`). `table capacity` above omits the
# label and the renderer falls back to the id; `""` here is a decision — this
# block is captioned by the prose that embeds it, and repeating the caption
# inside the figure is the duplication `TRANSCRIPTION-FIDELITY-TIERS` warns about. Absent says nothing;
# `""` says the author looked and chose none. Both are in the model, and a
# consumer that tests truthiness rather than key presence merges them.
table rings ""
# A `#` inside a pipe row is ORDINARY TEXT (core §1). A row is raw GFM content
# and no comment is stripped anywhere inside it, so `#0` below is a ring number
# — the same `#` after whitespace on a directive line would open a comment.
| Port     | Ring #0 | Ring #1 |
|----------|--------:|--------:|
| et-0/0/0 |    4096 |    4096 |
| et-0/0/1 |    2048 |    2048 |
